            "is_markdown": true,
            "count": 0,
            "bug_id": 19334,
            "text": "## Summary\n\nCalls with named arguments execute up to 9% fewer instructions when `matchArgs_NR()` compares argument names as symbols rather than as strings. Nice to have: a performance improvement with no change in behaviour.\n\n## Details\n\nThe exact-match pass of `matchArgs_NR()` (`src/main/match.c`) compares every formal with every tagged argument by `strcmp()` on their print names, about 35 instructions per pair. The patch tests `TAG(b) == ftag` instead. This is equivalent because `install()` keeps one symbol per name and the pass compares bytes without translation; names that differ only in encoding are still matched by `pmatch()` in the second pass. Calls without named arguments are unchanged, and this is independent of the positional fast path in PR#19309.\n\nTimings: attached as `matchargs-exact-bench.R`, which prints ns per call for eleven calls. On macOS arm64 (r90921, Apple clang 21), `f2(x = x, y = y)` goes from 170 to 156 ns and `rep(x, times = 2L)` from 101 to 98 ns; across 28 whole programs, instructions drop by 0.1% on average and 1.9% at most.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `matchargs-exact-symbol.diff`, against r90921. `make check` passes on macOS.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/503, with CI results and the full measurements.",
            "creation_time": "2026-11-17T18:38:15Z",
            "creator": "kevinushey"
          },
          {
